<div>
  <img decoding="async" width="696" height="392" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-768x432-1.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-post-image" alt="Study Shows 36% of Nigerian Adults Are Financially Excluded — Digital Financial Services Bolsters Inclusion" loading="lazy" style="float:left; margin:0 15px 15px 0;" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-768x432-1.jpg 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-300x169.jpg 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-696x392.jpg 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-1068x601.jpg 1068w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-747x420.jpg 747w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-190x107.jpg 190w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-380x214.jpg 380w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578-760x428.jpg 760w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/shutterstock_638489578.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 696px) 100vw, 696px" /></p> 
  
  <p>
    <strong>Nearly 36% of Nigeria’s 106 million adults lack access to both regulated and unregulated financial services, according to the findings of the Enhancing Financial Innovations and Access (EFIA) study. In addition, while the findings do suggest a marginal drop in the percentage of financially excluded adults in the past few years, overall “the actual number of financially excluded adults increased from 36.6 million to 38.1 million.”</strong>
  </p>
  
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">
    Population Growth Rate Faster Than Financial Inclusion Growth Rate
  </h2>
  
  <p>
    Additionally, the study findings show this number (financially excluded adults) rising to 52.5 million when not including those that gain access via the so-called informal or unregulated financial services.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <img decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="aligncenter wp-image-464255 size-full" title="Study Shows 36% of Nigerian Adults Are Financially Excluded — Digital Financial Services Bolsters Inclusion" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina.png" alt="Study Shows 36% of Nigerian Adults Are Financially Excluded — Digital Financial Services Bolsters Inclusion" width="1023" height="510" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina.png 1023w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina-300x150.png 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina-768x383.png 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina-696x347.png 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/efina-842x420.png 842w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 1023px" />
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Meanwhile, in its report, the EFIA attributes this mismatch (between the number of Nigerians that are unbanked (in percentage terms) and the actual number of excluded adults) to the population growth rate. In fact, according to EFIA, Nigeria’s population growth rate is currently outpacing “the rate of financial inclusion growth.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    On the other hand, when compared with past studies, the latest findings suggest that Nigeria’s “proportion of formally served adults increased for the first time since 2014.” Yet, despite this apparent drop in the number of unbanked adults, Nigeria still lags behind countries like South Africa and Rwanda where only 7% of the adult population is unbanked.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <img decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="aligncenter wp-image-464254 size-full" title="Study Shows 36% of Nigerian Adults Are Financially Excluded — Digital Financial Services Bolsters Inclusion" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/nigeria-financial-exclusion.png" alt="Study Shows 36% of Nigerian Adults Are Financially Excluded — Digital Financial Services Bolsters Inclusion" width="1023" height="406" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/nigeria-financial-exclusion.png 1023w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/nigeria-financial-exclusion-300x119.png 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/nigeria-financial-exclusion-768x305.png 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/nigeria-financial-exclusion-696x276.png 696w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 1023px" />
  </p>
  
  <p>
    As the findings of the study that was carried between November 2020 to February 2021 show, Kenya (11%) is the country with the next lowest number of adults that are unbanked while Burkina Faso (39%) has the highest number.
  </p>
  
  <p style="text-align: center;">
    <!-- growjs zone placement 31 -->
    
    <ins> <ins class="growjs-placement"></ins> </ins> <!-- end of growjs zone placement -->
  </p>
  
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">
    Importance of Digital Financial Services
  </h2>
  
  <p>
    Also of particular significance for advocates of emerging fintechs is the study’s findings asserting the growing importance of digital financial services in Nigeria. The EFIA study report states:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Growth in digital financial services, agent networks, and mobile phone ownership (now at 81%) highlights the opportunity to drive faster financial inclusion growth through digital financial services such as mobile money.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p>
    The study report, however, fails to share details of the actual digital financial services or products that Nigerians are using. Instead, the report seems to only discuss the potential impact of the increased uptake of mobile money on Nigeria’s financial inclusion targets.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <em><strong>What are your thoughts on the latest number of Nigerians that are unbanked? You can share your views in the comments section below. </strong></em>
  </p>
